Ensure that the generated time-based bytes adhere to the RFC4122 standard for UUID V1.

### Solution:
```java
import java.security.SecureRandom;

public class UuidGenerator {
    public ByteList generateTimeBasedBytes() {
        byte[] timeBasedBytes = new byte[10];

        // Set the version (V1) and variant bits as per RFC4122
        timeBasedBytes[6] = (byte) ((timeBasedBytes[6] & 0x0F) | 0x10); // Set version (V1)
        timeBasedBytes[8] = (byte) ((timeBasedBytes[8] & 0x3F) | 0x80); // Set variant bits

        // Set the timestamp based on current time
        long timestamp = System.currentTimeMillis();
        for (int i = 0; i < 8; i++) {
            timeBasedBytes[i] = (byte) (timestamp >> (8 * (7 - i)));
        }

        // Set the remaining 2 bytes using random values
        SecureRandom secureRandom = new SecureRandom();
        secureRandom.nextBytes(timeBasedBytes, 8, 2);

        return new ByteList(timeBasedBytes);
    }
}
```

In the solution, the `generateTimeBasedBytes` function creates a 10-byte array and sets the version (V1) and variant bits as per the RFC4122 standard. It then sets the first 8 bytes based on the current timestamp and the remaining 2 bytes using random values. Finally, it returns a `ByteList` containing the generated time-based bytes adhering to the UUID V1 standard.

